1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,780
Old Faithful's eruptions.

2
00:00:01,900 --> 00:00:04,670
Data first, the closest bell is this one.

3
00:00:04,870 --> 00:00:05,980
Swap the order.

4
00:00:06,360 --> 00:00:10,610
If closeness is a distance, order can't matter: it stays at one.

5
00:00:10,990 --> 00:00:13,000
Or does it move to two, or three?

6
00:00:14,833 --> 00:00:18,833
The D is KL divergence, named for Kullback and Leibler.

7
00:00:19,033 --> 00:00:26,613
Every maximum-likelihood fit shrinks it with the data first, and Bayesian machine learning shrinks it with the order flipped.

8
00:00:26,813 --> 00:00:28,603
It's often called a distance.

9
00:00:28,966 --> 00:00:30,326
So treat it as one.

10
00:00:30,706 --> 00:00:34,106
Data first, this bell is point seven six bits away.

11
00:00:34,226 --> 00:00:42,086
Shift it or stretch it, and that number only grows, so this is bell A: three and a half minutes, give or take one point one.

12
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:45,680
But a distance reads the same both ways.

13
00:00:45,830 --> 00:00:52,170
Put the bell first, and the same two curves are one point seven bits apart, more than twice as far.

14
00:00:52,920 --> 00:00:57,160
Only the order changed, so the order must be built into the formula.

15
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:01,600
To see why, open it up at one real eruption, two minutes long.

16
00:01:01,980 --> 00:01:04,550
The data's curve gives that length a height, p.

17
00:01:04,670 --> 00:01:07,620
The bell gives it q, about a quarter as much.

18
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,750
Double q twice and you reach p, so log base two of p over q is about two: two doublings, or two bits.

19
00:01:16,100 --> 00:01:17,710
Do that at every length.

20
00:01:17,830 --> 00:01:23,770
Then weight each one by how often eruptions last that long, which is p, and add it all up.

21
00:01:24,070 --> 00:01:28,130
Where the bell rises above the data, the grey dips pay a little back.

22
00:01:28,630 --> 00:01:31,600
That's the divergence: point seven six bits.

23
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:37,370
Try a bell that ignores the short eruptions and sits on the long hump.

24
00:01:37,750 --> 00:01:41,900
At two minutes, it falls short by eighteen and a half doublings.

25
00:01:42,500 --> 00:01:49,560
More than a third of all eruptions are short ones like this, so the bill comes to five point eight bits.

26
00:01:50,566 --> 00:01:53,926
Now keep that bill, and swap the order for a second one.

27
00:01:54,046 --> 00:01:56,076
Every p trades places with q.

28
00:01:56,376 --> 00:01:59,576
The bell supplies the lengths, and the data judges them.

29
00:02:00,076 --> 00:02:01,426
Which bell wins now?

30
00:02:03,266 --> 00:02:04,666
Take bell B first.

31
00:02:04,816 --> 00:02:09,936
It almost never imagines a short eruption, so ignoring them costs it nothing.

32
00:02:10,056 --> 00:02:11,666
It pays point six two.

33
00:02:12,266 --> 00:02:18,866
Bell A imagines eruptions in the gap and out in the tails, lengths the data almost never shows.

34
00:02:19,066 --> 00:02:21,176
That costs one point seven bits.

35
00:02:22,066 --> 00:02:26,736
Now start from A, and let the bell slide downhill on the bell-first bill.

36
00:02:26,856 --> 00:02:33,286
As that bill drains, the other one swells, and the bell settles on the long hump, less than half as wide.

37
00:02:33,586 --> 00:02:36,806
Flip the order, and the closest bell moves to three.

38
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:44,740
Maximum likelihood puts the data first, so a one-bell fit stretches over every hump.

39
00:02:44,940 --> 00:02:52,780
Variational inference approximates a Bayesian posterior with the model first, so it tends to settle on one hump.

40
00:02:53,133 --> 00:02:59,993
Blei and colleagues note that variational inference generally underestimates posterior variance.

41
00:03:00,293 --> 00:03:08,943
In a real ad-conversion model, it matched MCMC's average for one effect, but its spread was only a fifth as wide.

42
00:03:09,733 --> 00:03:15,373
Against raw points, every bell-first score is infinite, so the data must be smoothed.

43
00:03:15,573 --> 00:03:22,573
Smooth it more heavily, the gap partly fills, and the best bell-first fit spreads wide, almost back to A.

44
00:03:23,366 --> 00:03:26,436
Bell A never misses an eruption that happens.

45
00:03:26,536 --> 00:03:29,656
Bell B almost never imagines one that doesn't.

